{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 1–2 weeks show an upswing, add an upward bias to the whole path (model is typically ~+1 to +3 EUR/t too low for days ~5–25).",
    "Model reacts too slowly to upside breaks/spikes: after a sharp jump in the true series, lift the next 3–10 forecast days more aggressively (otherwise the model lags and stays below realized levels).",
    "Overestimation of late-horizon continuation: beyond ~day 20 the model often extrapolates gains too far (e.g., projecting 70–72 when realized mean-reverts to mid/high-60s). Damp the slope after day ~20.",
    "Mean reversion is under-modeled mid-horizon but over-modeled at the end: increase mid-horizon level in uptrends, but enforce stronger pullback toward the recent 30d mean in the final 5–10 days.",
    "Turning-point risk: when recent history shows a peak followed by softening, reduce the forecast level and volatility; the model tends to keep prices elevated for too long before the drop materializes."
  ]
}
